## Ascribe documentation

The Markdown pages under `handbook/pages/security/content/` are Ascribe documentation: pages with YAML frontmatter, `@` directives, and `{key}` phrases, checked against the content model in `handbook/pages/security/ascribe.toml`.

Programmatic check: after editing a page, run `ascribe check <file> --format concise` and fix every error. Before you finish, run `ascribe check --config handbook/pages/security`. `ascribe explain <code>` says what a problem means and how to fix it.

Before writing a page about a subject, run `ascribe search "<words>" --config handbook/pages/security` to find where the pages already say it, as readers read them, and link to what's there or extend it instead of saying it again.

### Page types

A page says what type it is with `type:` in its frontmatter, first, and its other fields are its type's. A file or folder whose name starts with `_` is a fragment, which pages `@include`, not a page, and names no type.

- `page`: frontmatter `title` (string, required), `description` (string)
- `policy`: frontmatter `title` (string, required), `owner` (string, required), `review` (enum(quarterly, yearly), required), `effective` (date, required), `review-by` (date)

### Phrases

Write the phrase (`{team}`), not the text it stands for.

- `{team}`: Security
- `{channel}`: #security
- `{report}`: security@lantern.example

### Directives this project uses

- `@id: <id>` on the line under a heading: an id for links to it that doesn't change with its text
- `@include: <file>.md`, or `@include: <file>.md#<id>` for one section: another file's content, in place
- `@note {type=<type>}: <text>`, or `@note:` … `@end` around several blocks: a callout. Here: types `note`, `tip`, `important`, `warning`, `caution`, `policy`
- `@steps` on the line above an ordered list: the list is a procedure
- `.<Title>` on its own line, then `@details:` … `@end`: collapsible content; the title is required
- `@term` on the line under a heading of a glossary page, with `{aliases="<a>|<b>"}` for other forms: the heading is a glossary term, defined by the paragraph under it

### Links

Link to the source file, with a relative path such as `../guides/install.md#configure`, not to the site's address. `ascribe outline <page>` lists a page's headings with their ids, and `ascribe link <target> --from <page>` says whether a link works and what to write.

### More

`ascribe model handbook/pages/security` shows everything the content model allows. Every directive: https://ascribed-dev.com/go/directives/
